ABOUT MONTYSOLR
===============

MontySolr is the search engine behind Astrophysics Data System (ADS 2.0) - 
"the world's most complete collection of scholarly content in the physical sciences" - 
http://ads.harvard.edu/. It is built on top of SOLR (http://lucene.apache.org/solr) 
but it is not a fork. New features are added as extensions. You can use MontySolr's 
infrastructure to add new functionality or test patches without duplicating code.

So what is here:

  - extremely powerful query parser (equivalent of which you won't find anywhere else)
  - second order queries (operators): analytical query operations
  - batch processing for long-running tasks
  - bitset queries (to search/analyze millions of docs by sending their IDs)
  - author name disambiguation
  - extensive tokenization rules
  - multi-synonym parsing and expansion
  - support for Jython
  - and of course: the whole configuration of the ADS search engine
  
  
In the past MontySolr combined Java with extensions written in C++/Python but now 
that Jython support for JNI arrived, MontySolr switched to pure Java (April 2014)
  


SEARCH CASE AND EXACTNESS
=========================

The ADS query modifier `=` suppresses synonym and expanded-name alternatives;
it does not request a literal search of the source text. For example,
`=full:SALT` avoids adding the “South African Large Telescope” expansion, but
can still match an ordinary lowercase use of “salt.”

The ADS text analyzer lowercases indexed term text and tokenizes punctuation,
but does not erase every distinction. It tags analyzer-classified acronym
terms and retains compound-token paths alongside their component terms.
Ordinary queries can still merge hyphenated and concatenated spellings, so
these paths do not provide literal punctuation matching. Case can affect query
classification; `=` does not turn off analysis or provide a literal-case mode.

With the ADS AQP parser, a field regular-expression query is matched against
indexed terms directly, without analyzing the regex as body text. For example,
`body:/acr::salt/` targets the indexed body term `acr::salt` (and does not scan
stored body text). The `acr::` prefix is an analyzer-added tag that marks a
token classified as an acronym. This can be useful for targeting tagged terms
in the current index, but it is an analyzer detail rather than a stable
acronym-metadata API or a guarantee that the source contained an acronym:
uppercase synonym outputs may receive the same tag. Such a query can therefore
match an expansion derived from lowercase source text, and its result is
limited to the field named in the query.

Neither `=` nor an `acr::` term query guarantees the original letter case or
punctuation in the source. A strict source-spelling search requires searchable
data that preserves that spelling; the existing ADS text terms do not provide
that guarantee.

INSTALLATION
============

Go ahead to http://github.com/adsabs/releases and download/unzip the version of your
choice. Then run ./bin/solr

If you want to build Montysolr from sources, rest assured it is easy.
You will need Java JDK (version 11 and higher)

```bash

 $ git clone https://github.com/adsabs/montysolr
 $ cd montysolr
 $ ./gradlew build
```



